J-unit five, and parameterised tests.

Code you can't trust to change is code that's already dying. Automated tests are how you keep changing a program without breaking it — a suite you run on every edit that tells you, in seconds, still works, or, you broke this. In Java, the standard framework for writing those tests is J-unit five. This section is how it works, plus the feature that keeps test code small: parameterised tests.

A J-unit test is just a method marked with the test annotation. Inside, you exercise your code and make assertions — checks that throw, and fail the test, if reality doesn't match expectation. The universal shape is arrange, act, assert. So: arrange by creating a calculator; act by calling add of two and three; and assert that the sum equals five. If it isn't five, the assertion fails loudly. And recall from this module's opening — J-unit finds these methods by scanning your classes for the test annotation, using reflection. That's the exact mechanism from the annotations section, now doing real work.

The assertion library covers what you need to state expectations precisely: assert-equals, assert-true, assert-null, and assert-throws — for the important case of checking that code fails correctly. For instance, assert that withdrawing a negative amount throws an illegal-argument exception; a bad withdrawal must throw. And for setup and cleanup, lifecycle annotations run around your tests. Before-each and after-each run before and after every single test — giving you fresh fixtures, with no shared state. Before-all and after-all run once for the whole class, for expensive, one-time setup.

Now, often you want to check the same logic against a dozen inputs. Copy-pasting the test a dozen times is just noise. A parameterised test runs one test method once per input, each reported as its own case. So you mark the method as a parameterised test, give it a value source — say, the words racecar, level, and noon — and assert each one is a palindrome. The input source is pluggable: value-source for a simple list, C-S-V source for input-plus-expected pairs, and method-source when the cases need code to generate them. One method, broad coverage — and when something breaks, the failure pinpoints the exact input that broke it.

The framework is easy; the discipline is the point. A good test checks one behavior, so a failure names the thing that broke. Its name says what it verifies — adds-two-numbers, not test-one — so a red result reads like a sentence. And it's fast and deterministic, giving the same result every run, because a flaky test that fails at random trains you to ignore failures, which defeats the entire purpose. Write tests as you write the code, and the suite becomes the safety net that lets you refactor fearlessly.

One gap remains. Real classes don't stand alone — they depend on databases, network calls, other services — and you can't unit-test a class quickly or deterministically while it's really talking to a database. The last section closes that gap: replacing a unit's collaborators with controllable stand-ins, using Mockito.
